  "answer_core": "I would not grant Marcus 15% equity, a CTO title, and a board seat on Friday. That is too permanent for an unproven dependency system.\n\nThe real issue is not whether he is worth 15% in the abstract. Several dependencies may run through him at once: platform prototype, technical delivery, Jake and Lina, client confidence, and part of the exit story. If you grant the full package now, you price contribution and concentration risk before knowing whether Marcus strengthens the company beyond himself or increases that risk.\n\nSo Friday should be a serious, direct, structured conversation about a staged partnership—not vague delay or flat rejection. A vague “later” can read as avoidance and trigger exactly what you are trying to prevent: Marcus disengaging, pulling others with him, slowing the platform, or destabilizing client continuity.\n\nYour position: there may be a path to major upside and leadership, but not before evidence. Define a short sprint that tests platform delivery, revenue relevance, team continuity, knowledge transfer, governance behavior, and whether he strengthens the company beyond himself. Spell out what success in that window would unlock, keep intermediate structures alive, but do not turn the answer into a catalog of instruments.\n\nUse exit math only as false-precision caution. Until buyer assumptions, platform value, and departure scenarios are tested, the numbers should not carry the decision.\n\nGo in ready to say you want to explore a serious path, but cannot make irreversible governance and ownership commitments before proving what the partnership does to the company.",
